{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-7:202\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The verse depicts companions extending others in error without relenting, supplying a social mechanism by which people may remain astray.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"7:202\",\"ar\":\"يَمُدُّونَهُمْ فِى ٱلْغَىِّ ثُمَّ لَا يُقْصِرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus ends by excluding ٱلضَّآلِّينَ from the requested path.\",\"limits\":\"This uses الغي rather than ضلل and does not identify these companions with the focus’s excluded class.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-8:53\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The people once given a نعمة can change what is within themselves, after which God changes that favor; favor-receiving is not presented as an immutable communal status.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"8:53\",\"ar\":\"لَمْ يَكُ مُغَيِّرًۭا نِّعْمَةً أَنْعَمَهَا عَلَىٰ قَوْمٍ حَتَّىٰ يُغَيِّرُوا۟ مَا بِأَنفُسِهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names a community through أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ and immediately distinguishes it from excluded groups.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not say that the people whose favor changes thereby become المغضوب عليهم or الضالين.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:93\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The adjacent contrast distinguishes inability from blameworthy refusal: the tearful people unable to contribute are set apart from wealthy people who seek permission to stay behind, upon whom liability falls.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"9:92\",\"ar\":\"وَأَعْيُنُهُمْ تَفِيضُ مِنَ ٱلدَّمْعِ حَزَنًا أَلَّا يَجِدُوا۟ مَا يُنفِقُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"9:93\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّمَا ٱلسَّبِيلُ عَلَى ٱلَّذِينَ يَسْتَـْٔذِنُونَكَ وَهُمْ أَغْنِيَآءُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus distinguishes the favored path from excluded people without specifying the conduct that separates them.\",\"limits\":\"Here السَّبِيلُ عَلَى marks liability, not a route, and the passage does not name the Fatiha’s classes.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-9:115\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse places divine misguidance after guidance and after clarification of what the people should avoid, giving an order of disclosure before being led astray.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"9:115\",\"ar\":\"وَمَا كَانَ ٱللَّهُ لِيُضِلَّ قَوْمًۢا بَعْدَ إِذْ هَدَىٰهُمْ حَتَّىٰ يُبَيِّنَ لَهُم مَّا يَتَّقُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus addresses God as the one who bestowed favor and names astray people without explaining how they reached that state.\",\"limits\":\"This states a condition of divine misguidance; it does not identify the people named in either passage as the same group.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:15\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The same word غَيْر marks a requested Quran other than the one recited, while in the focus it marks the excluded classes relative to the requested path.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ\"},{\"ref\":\"10:15\",\"ar\":\"ٱئْتِ بِقُرْءَانٍ غَيْرِ هَٰذَآ أَوْ بَدِّلْهُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s غَيْرِ is the hinge between the favored group and the excluded groups.\",\"limits\":\"The constructions and situations differ; this does not make the Quran rejected in 10:15 equivalent to either excluded class.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:23\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"People rescued from danger then act unjustly on earth, showing a change in conduct after a divine deliverance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"10:23\",\"ar\":\"فَلَمَّآ أَنجَىٰهُمْ إِذَا هُمْ يَبْغُونَ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ بِغَيْرِ ٱلْحَقِّ\"}],\"activation\":\"This sequence makes the focus’s favor-recipient class legible alongside its excluded classes as a distinction that conduct after favor can put under pressure.\",\"limits\":\"The passage calls the later conduct بغي; it does not say these people were previously among the focus’s favored or later became المغضوب عليهم.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:25\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse joins God’s call to the Abode of Peace with guidance to a straight path, a close parallel to the focus’s specification of the requested route.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"10:25\",\"ar\":\"وَٱللَّهُ يَدْعُوٓا۟ إِلَىٰ دَارِ ٱلسَّلَٰمِ وَيَهْدِى مَن يَشَآءُ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus completes its request for a path by naming the people associated with it and those outside it.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not give the same list of people or exclusions as the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:32\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse sets الضلال against recognized truth and asks how people are turned away, making the focus’s final excluded class part of a sharp truth-versus-deviation contrast.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"10:32\",\"ar\":\"فَمَاذَا بَعْدَ ٱلْحَقِّ إِلَّا ٱلضَّلَٰلُ ۖ فَأَنَّىٰ تُصْرَفُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"الضَّآلِّينَ closes the focus with the same root, ض ل ل, as الضَّلَٰلُ here.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not specify that every use of the root has precisely the same scope or referents.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:62\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The next verse identifies God’s allies as believers who practice taqwa and describes them as free of fear and grief; this may illuminate the positive community implied by the focus’s favored people.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"10:62\",\"ar\":\"أَلَآ إِنَّ أَوْلِيَآءَ ٱللَّهِ لَا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"10:63\",\"ar\":\"ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَكَانُوا۟ يَتَّقُونَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The passage does not identify these allies with ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ; an explicit Quranic link between the two descriptions could establish whether they are parallel groups.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-10:108\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Guidance benefits the person who follows it, while straying bears upon the person who strays; the route’s distinction has personal consequences.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"10:108\",\"ar\":\"فَمَنِ ٱهْتَدَىٰ فَإِنَّمَا يَهْتَدِى لِنَفْسِهِۦ ۖ وَمَن ضَلَّ فَإِنَّمَا يَضِلُّ عَلَيْهَا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus prays in the plural for a path and ends by excluding ٱلضَّآلِّينَ.\",\"limits\":\"This verse locates consequence with each person but does not explain the focus’s description of divine favor or anger.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-11:19\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"One described group actively blocks سبيل الله and seeks to make it crooked; the excluded path’s alternatives can therefore include opposition that tries to alter the route for others.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"11:19\",\"ar\":\"ٱلَّذِينَ يَصُدُّونَ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ وَيَبْغُونَهَا عِوَجًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names a path and excludes groups, while this verse supplies a picture of people working against God’s way.\",\"limits\":\"The focus does not say that all of the excluded people obstruct the path or seek to distort it.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-11:37\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-11:56\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"In the setting of God’s grasp of every creature, Hūd says his Lord is upon a straight path; this places straightness also in the Lord’s governing relation to creation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"11:56\",\"ar\":\"مَّا مِن دَآبَّةٍ إِلَّا هُوَ ءَاخِذٌۢ بِنَاصِيَتِهَآ ۚ إِنَّ رَبِّى عَلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"This repeats the focus’s path image and the صراط مستقيم wording of the request immediately preceding it in the Fatiha.\",\"limits\":\"The verse predicates this path of the Lord in a statement about His governance; it does not directly define the human community sought in the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-11:112\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Steadfastness as commanded is paired with a ban on overstepping; the following verse warns against inclining toward wrongdoers and names the fire as the consequence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"11:112\",\"ar\":\"فَٱسْتَقِمْ كَمَآ أُمِرْتَ وَمَن تَابَ مَعَكَ وَلَا تَطْغَوْا۟\"},{\"ref\":\"11:113\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا تَرْكَنُوٓا۟ إِلَى ٱلَّذِينَ ظَلَمُوا۟ فَتَمَسَّكُمُ ٱلنَّارُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus asks for the path and excludes groups; this passage turns uprightness into a commanded practice with a boundary against alliance with wrongdoers.\",\"limits\":\"The imperative اسْتَقِمْ is not the noun صِرَاطَ, and the passage does not equate its wrongdoers with the focus’s named classes.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:6\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Joseph’s favor is described as completed upon him and Jacob’s family, with a remembered line reaching Abraham and Isaac; favor can mark a family across generations.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"12:6\",\"ar\":\"وَيُتِمُّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكَ وَعَلَىٰٓ ءَالِ يَعْقُوبَ كَمَآ أَتَمَّهَا عَلَىٰٓ أَبَوَيْكَ مِن قَبْلُ إِبْرَٰهِيمَ وَإِسْحَٰقَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus likewise names a plural community through divine favor upon them.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not identify Jacob’s family with every person meant by ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:8\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The brothers call their father “in clear error” while plotting to remove Joseph; this shows the ض ل ل root can name an accuser’s mistaken judgment, not solely a settled religious class.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"12:8\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ أَبَانَا لَفِى ضَلَٰلٍۢ مُّبِينٍ\"},{\"ref\":\"12:9\",\"ar\":\"ٱقْتُلُوا۟ يُوسُفَ أَوِ ٱطْرَحُوهُ أَرْضًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus ends with the related form ٱلضَّآلِّينَ but leaves the object and manner of their straying unstated.\",\"limits\":\"The narrative use does not determine the intended sense of the focus’s plural label by itself.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:57\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The surah associates a better afterlife reward with people who believe and practice taqwa, offering one profile of a positively rewarded community.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"12:57\",\"ar\":\"وَلَأَجْرُ ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ خَيْرٌۭ لِّلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَكَانُوا۟ يَتَّقُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus identifies a sought community through favor and sets it against excluded groups.\",\"limits\":\"This verse does not call the believers and God-fearing the favored people of the Fatiha or describe their path.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:71\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-12:95\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Joseph’s brothers call Jacob’s expectation his old ضلال, but the messenger’s arrival and Jacob’s restored sight expose their judgment as mistaken; the root can carry an accusation later overturned by events.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"12:95\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّكَ لَفِى ضَلَٰلِكَ ٱلْقَدِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"12:96\",\"ar\":\"فَلَمَّآ أَن جَآءَ ٱلْبَشِيرُ أَلْقَىٰهُ عَلَىٰ وَجْهِهِۦ فَٱرْتَدَّ بَصِيرًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"As in the focus, the root ض ل ل is used for a classifying judgment about a person, though here it is the brothers’ judgment of their father.\",\"limits\":\"This episode does not establish that every use of ضلال in the Quran is an erroneous accusation.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The revelation is said to bring people from darkness into light and then to the path of the Mighty, the Praiseworthy; the route requested in the focus is framed elsewhere as an exit from darkness into divine guidance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"14:1\",\"ar\":\"لِتُخْرِجَ ٱلنَّاسَ مِنَ ٱلظُّلُمَٰتِ إِلَى ٱلنُّورِ بِإِذْنِ رَبِّهِمْ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطِ ٱلْعَزِيزِ ٱلْحَمِيدِ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages use صراط, and the focus distinguishes the people associated with that path from excluded groups.\",\"limits\":\"This verse does not list the favored and excluded communities named in the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:3\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Those who prefer this life, block God’s way, and seek crookedness are explicitly described as being in distant ضلال; this ties straying to a chosen relation to the path.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"14:3\",\"ar\":\"يَسْتَحِبُّونَ ٱلْحَيَوٰةَ ٱلدُّنْيَا عَلَى ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ وَيَصُدُّونَ عَن سَبِيلِ ٱللَّهِ وَيَبْغُونَهَا عِوَجًا ۚ أُو۟لَٰئِكَ فِى ضَلَٰلٍۭ بَعِيدٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus couples its path image with the final label ٱلضَّآلِّينَ.\",\"limits\":\"This verse gives a particular profile, not a complete definition of everyone called astray in the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:4\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Messengers speak in their people’s language to clarify, followed by a statement that God guides whom He wills and leads astray whom He wills.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"14:4\",\"ar\":\"بِلِسَانِ قَوْمِهِۦ لِيُبَيِّنَ لَهُمْ ۖ فَيُضِلُّ ٱللَّهُ مَن يَشَآءُ وَيَهْدِى مَن يَشَآءُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus addresses God directly as giver of favor and names people who are astray.\",\"limits\":\"This verse states both clarification and divine will but does not spell out how those elements relate in each case.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:6\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Moses identifies God’s favor upon his people with their rescue from Pharaoh’s oppression, making favor a remembered communal deliverance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"14:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱذْكُرُوا۟ نِعْمَةَ ٱللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ إِذْ أَنجَىٰكُم مِّنْ ءَالِ فِرْعَوْنَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus uses the verb أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ for the community on the requested path.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not equate the rescued Israelites with the entire group intended by the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:7\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The passage moves from remembered favor to a conditional response: gratitude brings increase, while disbelief is met with severe punishment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"14:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱذْكُرُوا۟ نِعْمَةَ ٱللَّهِ عَلَيْكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:7\",\"ar\":\"لَئِن شَكَرْتُمْ لَأَزِيدَنَّكُمْ ۖ وَلَئِن كَفَرْتُمْ إِنَّ عَذَابِى لَشَدِيدٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus places favor and two excluded classes in one compact description, without narrating what follows receipt of favor.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not say that every ungrateful person becomes one of the focus’s wrath-incurred or astray.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:27\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"God’s making believers firm is set against His leading wrongdoers astray, a paired divine action that gives the focus’s contrast a dynamic form.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"14:27\",\"ar\":\"يُثَبِّتُ ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ بِٱلْقَوْلِ ٱلثَّابِتِ فِى ٱلْحَيَوٰةِ ٱلدُّنْيَا وَفِى ٱلْءَاخِرَةِ ۖ وَيُضِلُّ ٱللَّهُ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus contrasts a favored community with the astray and wrath-incurred, while this verse explicitly sets firmness against misguidance.\",\"limits\":\"The verse names believers and wrongdoers, not the exact classes named in the Fatiha.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:28\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"A people exchange God’s favor for disbelief and bring their people to a ruinous abode; this supplies a concrete reversal from favor to rejection and loss.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"14:28\",\"ar\":\"ٱلَّذِينَ بَدَّلُوا۟ نِعْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ كُفْرًۭا وَأَحَلُّوا۟ قَوْمَهُمْ دَارَ ٱلْبَوَارِ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:29\",\"ar\":\"جَهَنَّمَ يَصْلَوْنَهَا ۖ وَبِئْسَ ٱلْقَرَارُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names people through God’s favor and then marks other classes outside their path.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not identify its people as the same recipients named in the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:36\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Abraham says idols have led many astray, distinguishes those who follow him from those who disobey, yet entrusts the disobedient to God’s forgiveness and mercy; disobedience is not here equated automatically with a settled final category.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"14:36\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ إِنَّهُنَّ أَضْلَلْنَ كَثِيرًۭا مِّنَ ٱلنَّاسِ ۖ فَمَن تَبِعَنِى فَإِنَّهُۥ مِنِّى ۖ وَمَنْ عَصَانِى فَإِنَّكَ غَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus distinguishes a path-associated people from astray and wrath-incurred groups, while Abraham’s prayer holds disobedience and possible mercy together.\",\"limits\":\"Abraham’s appeal does not state that every disobedient person is forgiven or define the focus’s groups.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-14:48\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The same word غَيْر appears in a scene where the earth is replaced by another earth, while the focus uses it to delimit the people associated with the path.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ\"},{\"ref\":\"14:48\",\"ar\":\"يَوْمَ تُبَدَّلُ ٱلْأَرْضُ غَيْرَ ٱلْأَرْضِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s exclusion hinge and this replacement clause share the lexeme غَيْر.\",\"limits\":\"The otherness in this scene concerns the earth on the Day of Judgment; it does not make that earth an alternative path or class.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:39\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Satan declares an intention to adorn earthly life and lead people astray, with the next verse excepting God’s sincere servants; the passage depicts a source of misleading influence and a protected exception.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"15:39\",\"ar\":\"لَأُزَيِّنَنَّ لَهُمْ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ وَلَأُغْوِيَنَّهُمْ أَجْمَعِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:40\",\"ar\":\"إِلَّا عِبَادَكَ مِنْهُمُ ٱلْمُخْلَصِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus excludes ٱلضَّآلِّينَ from the requested path, and this scene names an adversarial effort to lead people astray.\",\"limits\":\"This is Satan’s declared program, not a statement that his account of how he was misled is endorsed.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:41\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"After Satan’s declaration, God says هَٰذَا صِرَٰطٌ عَلَىَّ مُسْتَقِيمٌ, closely joining a straight path with the preceding dispute over misleading and protected servants.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"15:41\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ هَٰذَا صِرَٰطٌ عَلَىَّ مُسْتَقِيمٌ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:40\",\"ar\":\"إِلَّا عِبَادَكَ مِنْهُمُ ٱلْمُخْلَصِينَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The excerpt does not settle what عَلَيَّ contributes here or what هَٰذَا points back to; grammatical and contextual evidence from the surrounding passage could decide whether the phrase stresses a path under God’s claim, a promise, or another relation.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:42\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"God’s servants are beyond Satan’s authority except those who follow him among the astray, with Hell named as their promised destination in the next verse.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"15:42\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ عِبَادِى لَيْسَ لَكَ عَلَيْهِمْ سُلْطَٰنٌ إِلَّا مَنِ ٱتَّبَعَكَ مِنَ ٱلْغَاوِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"15:43\",\"ar\":\"وَإِنَّ جَهَنَّمَ لَمَوْعِدُهُمْ أَجْمَعِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus both names a sought community and excludes the astray; this passage likewise sets servants apart from followers of the adversary.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not identify these servants or followers with the Fatiha’s classes.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-15:87\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The mention of seven oft-repeated units makes the seven-verse Fatiha a plausible referent; if so, 1:7 closes the named recited unit.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"15:87\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ ءَاتَيْنَٰكَ سَبْعًۭا مِّنَ ٱلْمَثَانِى\"},{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"صِرَٰطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"}],\"missing\":\"This verse does not name the seven as the Fatiha; evidence identifying the referent would establish whether the focus functions as the ending of the seven oft-repeated.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:5\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:9\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse contrasts the path’s قَصْد with a crooked way and says God could have guided everyone; it presents deviation as a real alternative to an upright course.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"16:9\",\"ar\":\"وَعَلَى ٱللَّهِ قَصْدُ ٱلسَّبِيلِ وَمِنْهَا جَآئِرٌۭ ۚ وَلَوْ شَآءَ لَهَدَىٰكُمْ أَجْمَعِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus asks for صراط and excludes those who stray, while this verse explicitly contrasts a directed path with crookedness.\",\"limits\":\"The phrase قَصْدُ ٱلسَّبِيلِ does not itself identify the people named in the focus.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:18\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"God’s favors are beyond enumeration, with forgiveness and mercy named; this broadens the scale of favor but supplies no particular path community.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"16:18\",\"ar\":\"وَإِن تَعُدُّوا۟ نِعْمَةَ ٱللَّهِ لَا تُحْصُوهَآ ۗ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَغَفُورٌۭ رَّحِيمٌۭ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus identifies people by the favor God bestowed upon them.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not connect these uncountable favors to the focus’s path or excluded groups.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:53\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Every favor is from God, yet after distress is removed a group associates partners with Him; receiving divine favor does not guarantee a faithful response.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"16:53\",\"ar\":\"وَمَا بِكُم مِّن نِّعْمَةٍۢ فَمِنَ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"16:54\",\"ar\":\"ثُمَّ إِذَا كَشَفَ ٱلضُّرَّ عَنكُمْ إِذَا فَرِيقٌۭ مِّنكُم بِرَبِّهِمْ يُشْرِكُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus places the recipients of favor beside classes excluded from the requested path.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not state that the later associators are the same people as the focus’s favor-receivers.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:72\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Provision of spouses, children, descendants, and good things is followed by a charge that people disbelieve in God’s favor, giving a household scale to failure to acknowledge نعمة.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"16:72\",\"ar\":\"وَرَزَقَكُم مِّنَ ٱلطَّيِّبَٰتِ ۚ أَفَبِٱلْبَٰطِلِ يُؤْمِنُونَ وَبِنِعْمَتِ ٱللَّهِ هُمْ يَكْفُرُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s favored people are defined by their relation to divine favor.\",\"limits\":\"This verse does not identify those who disbelieve in favor with either of the focus’s excluded classes.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:76\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The person who commands justice is explicitly said to be upon a straight path, associating the path with just conduct.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"16:76\",\"ar\":\"وَمَن يَأْمُرُ بِٱلْعَدْلِ ۙ وَهُوَ عَلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"This repeats the straight-path formula immediately requested before the focus specifies its people.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not directly state that commanding justice defines every member of the Fatiha’s favored community.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:81\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"After listing protections and provisions, the verse says God completes His favor upon people so that they may submit; favor is oriented toward a response.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"16:81\",\"ar\":\"كَذَٰلِكَ يُتِمُّ نِعْمَتَهُۥ عَلَيْكُمْ لَعَلَّكُمْ تُسْلِمُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names people through favor bestowed upon them and asks for their path.\",\"limits\":\"The verse expresses a hoped-for response, not that every recipient submits or joins the focus’s favored group.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:106\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Anger from God is explicitly attached to one who disbelieves after faith, except when compelled while the heart remains secure in faith; the passage supplies an example where inward assent and coercion matter.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"16:106\",\"ar\":\"إِلَّا مَنْ أُكْرِهَ وَقَلْبُهُۥ مُطْمَئِنٌّۢ بِٱلْإِيمَٰنِ وَلَٰكِن مَّن شَرَحَ بِٱلْكُفْرِ صَدْرًۭا فَعَلَيْهِمْ غَضَبٌۭ مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ without naming the agent or grounds of the anger.\",\"limits\":\"This is a specific case of divine anger, not a complete definition of the focus’s plural class.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-16:121\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Abraham is grateful for God’s favors, then chosen and guided to a straight path; gratitude, selection, and guidance occur as one linked portrait.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"16:121\",\"ar\":\"شَاكِرًۭا لِّأَنْعُمِهِ ۚ ٱجْتَبَىٰهُ وَهَدَىٰهُ إِلَىٰ صِرَٰطٍۢ مُّسْتَقِيمٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus identifies a path through the people favored by God, while this verse joins favor and guidance to a named exemplar.\",\"limits\":\"Abraham exemplifies the relationship but is not stated to exhaust the focus’s plural community.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The repeated صراط makes 1:7 an immediate specification of the path requested in 1:6: the prayer moves from asking for a straight path to identifying it through a favored community and exclusions.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"صِرَٰطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The path noun recurs at the head of the next verse, continuing the same petition.\",\"limits\":\"This identifies the relation between the two verses but does not by itself determine the full membership of each named class.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
